WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
Three ways to change what a model does.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:07.256
Every team that ships an LLM feature eventually asks: should we fine-tune?

3
00:00:07.536 --> 00:00:11.120
To answer well, be precise about what each option changes.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.000
Prompting changes the input.

5
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.120
You write instructions and examples; the model’s weights stay the same.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:25.784
Retrieval (Studio 2) also changes the input, by fetching relevant documents at question time and putting them in the context.

7
00:00:26.064 --> 00:00:28.261
Fine-tuning changes the model itself.

8
00:00:28.541 --> 00:00:39.357
In supervised fine-tuning (SFT), you show the model many input and ideal-output pairs and continue training, nudging its weights so that it produces outputs like yours.

9
00:00:39.637 --> 00:00:41.344
You have seen SFT before.

10
00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:52.248
In Studio 1, lesson 1, it was the first stage of post-training: the step that turns a document-completer into an assistant by training on example conversations.

11
00:00:52.528 --> 00:00:57.072
When you fine-tune, you run a small version of that same step on your own examples.

12
00:00:57.352 --> 00:00:59.763
That framing explains most of what follows.

13
00:01:00.043 --> 00:01:06.080
Post-training doesn’t teach a model the world’s facts (pretraining did that); it teaches it how to behave.

14
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.883
Your fine-tuning run is the same kind of tool, aimed at a narrower behavior.

15
00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:23.728
This week asks the question in the order a careful engineer would: what fine-tuning changes (this lesson), how to build the dataset it needs (lesson 5), and how to keep that data honest (lesson 6).

16
00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:26.611
Next week you run the training itself with LoRA.
